{"id":"by-q44s.1.55","title":"Beryl 3D holds 344 MB of GPU memory for its first second","description":"Symptom: `beryl3d --window none --for 8s` (release build with the `tools` feature, 2026-11-15, Apple M5 Max) reports a physical footprint of 360 MB from its first sample at 80 ms until about 1.3 s, of which 344 MB is 'Owned physical footprint (unmapped) (graphics)' in `footprint <pid>`. After that the footprint is 37 MB, 24 MB of it the GPU's. `/usr/bin/time -l` gives a peak memory footprint of 377 to 379 MB on five runs. docs/beryl-3d.md 'Card faces' allows about 32 MB of face textures for a match, and the floor (decision C2) includes the iPhone 6s with 2 GB of memory, where a 344 MB transient could get the app killed.

Where to look: `Renderer::new` and `set_faces` in rust/beryl3d/crates/beryl_render/src/renderer.rs and `face_arrays` / `FaceArray::new` in src/faces.rs (the placeholder writes for every layer, the full-size array of `FULL_LAYERS` layers at 672 by 936), the glyph atlas (src/atlas.rs), and the app's startup in rust/beryl3d/crates/beryl_3d/src/app.rs. Sample `footprint <pid>` every few milliseconds over the first 1.5 s to find which allocation it is.

