{"id":"by-q44s.35","title":"Enact gave one worktree slot to two beads after an app restart","description":"What happened: after the Claude app crashed and restarted, the vizier asked by-q44s.3.19's session to resume. Its worktree was /Users/dthurn/beryl/.worktrees/slot-08 with uncommitted work. Enact then also assigned slot-08 to by-q44s.29 (bd show by-q44s.29: enact_worktree = .worktrees/slot-08), and that session checked out enact/by-q44s.29 there while 3.19 was in the middle of a rebase (git reflog in slot-08: 'checkout: moving from 96709ae5d to enact/by-q44s.29' between 'rebase (start)' and 'rebase (finish)'). The rebase finished with no commit of 3.19's, and the next amend rewrote by-q44s.29's tip commit with 3.19's message. 3.19's commit survived only in the reflog (6a54b8ca5); it was moved to .worktrees/by-q44s.3.19 by hand and slot-08 was put back on enact/by-q44s.29. No work was lost, but about half an hour went into finding and undoing it, and it could easily have lost uncommitted work.

Why (to confirm): slot allocation seems to treat a slot as free when its owning session is not running, as during an app restart, without checking that the slot has an in-progress rebase, uncommitted changes or a branch that is still the bead's enact_branch.

