{"id":"by-q44s.7.22","title":"Handle phone taps, holds and drags in the scene","description":"Read docs/beryl-3d.md “Motion the pointer drives”, “Hosts” and “Interface checks”. beryl_host Phone::pointer and beryl_3d src/ffi/types.rs pointer_from_c already forward pointer IDs and logical positions. Keep gesture state in beryl_scene src/pointer (add touch.rs); route it through src/client/input.rs Client::point and the existing pointer::update/let_go, RowGesture::handle, Client::press and client/inspect.rs Inspector. Use one active gameplay finger; no hover or card raise for touch. Follow docs/ui-conventions.md “The phone”: a tap on a card the prompt can use follows its primary action, while an otherwise unhandled tap opens full-card inspection (including the hand). Tapping outside dismisses it. Use the web Pointer's #click unhandled viewer fallback and keep the hand drag available for casting. A stationary 500 ms hold opens the card inspector for gameplay or the existing secondary/menu action in the manual table; the release must not also select. Movement beyond 6 points cancels the hold. Engine hand drags can begin immediately after the movement threshold; manual-table drags of cards that support secondary menus have the 150 ms pickup rule. Drag a scrolling row instead of playing a card, preserve drop-to-play and return-to-hand behavior, and cancel all pending state on cancel/leave or layout/screen change. Route surface-triggered cancellation through the Input::Surface branch of Client::handle; the furniture bead owns Client::resize. Placement owns the 44-point strips, which touch must hit through the same resolved frame geometry. Keep any per-pointer state bounded by existing pointer IDs. Use the explicit stepped clock, existing Wake::or_at and pending input/command path; do not allocate or rebuild layout in a frame to show a held press.

